WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.600
This is one of the most interesting videos

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.540
based on classification of Rational numbers.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.240
We know that they are either terminating decimals

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.480
or non-terminating recurring decimal numbers.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.380
We also know that they can be expressed as

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.400
'p divided by q' where 'q' is not equal to zero.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.660
But just by looking at a fraction,

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.500
can you tell if it's terminating or non-terminating recurring?

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.820
Let's see! We look at 6 rational numbers in the 'p by q' form.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.520
'5 by 2'.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:48.880
'17 by 5'.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.620
'8 by 15'.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.340
'9 by 20'.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.100
'17 by 6'.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.060
'11 by 21'.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.400
Without calculating the value of each in decimal form,

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.860
can you directly tell the category each of them falls into?

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.620
This category or this one?

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.600
Let's make three rows and analyse it.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.240
Let this be the row of our numbers.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.980
Just for our understanding,

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:16.520
let us calculate the decimal values of each in this row.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.800
And we write the form of each decimal in this row.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.940
By form I mean either this or this.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.120
We will write 'T' if it's terminating

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.120
and 'NTR' if it's non-terminating recurring.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.880
Let's try calculating the decimal values of each of the numbers first.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.060
'5 by 2' can be written as '2 point 5'.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.700
So we can say that it is terminating.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.220
The digits after the decimal point end

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.260
and do not go on forever.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.840
'17 by 5' equals 3.4, which is also terminating.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:59.520
'8 by 15' equals 0.53333 and so on.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.360
It does not terminate.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.600
The digit 3 is repeated indefinitely.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.940
So we can say that it is a non-terminating recurring decimal.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:14.400
'9 by 20' equals 0.45, which is terminating.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.720
So what we are doing here is calculating the decimal value

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:18.860
of all the numbers

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.880
and classifying it into terminating

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.940
and non-terminating recurring decimals.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.280
Only two more numbers remain.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:32.080
'17 by 6' equals 2.833333 and so on.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.720
So correct, it gets classified as non-terminating recurring fraction.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:47.840
And '11 by 21' equals 0.523809523809 and so on.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.080
This part is repeated.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.180
So it's a non-terminating recurring fraction.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.980
Imagine trying to do all of this without a calculator.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.220
That's how your eyes would look, stunned!

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.480
Don't worry, there's a quicker way.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.560
All we need to do is look at the denominator.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.360
Yes, even if we just look at the denominator,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.940
we can figure out if it's a terminating or non-terminating recurring.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.680
The denominator of the first number is two.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.100
And the denominator of the second number is five.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.960
We just need to keep a small thing in mind.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.580
We reduce the denominators to its prime factors.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.140
The denominator of the third number is fifteen.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:34.100
In terms of its prime factors it can be written as 'three times five'.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:35.860
What about twenty?

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:39.400
It can be written as 'two squared multiplied by five'.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.100
Six can be written as '2 times 3'.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.680
And 21 can be written as 'three times seven'.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:48.760
That's all we had to do.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.760
Reduce the denominators to its prime factors.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.240
Let's observe the denominators of the terminating decimals.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.700
The prime factors of the denominator

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:02.700
are either two or five or both two and five.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.520
Now let's look at the prime factors of the denominators

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.220
of non-terminating recurring decimals.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.520
They have numbers other than two and five.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.580
Yes, that's the trick.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:19.160
If the prime factors of the denominator are two or five or both,

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.540
then the decimal is terminating.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.040
If the denominator contains any prime factor

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.140
other than two or five,

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.120
then it's a non-terminating recurring decimal.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:32.280
These are the only two rules you need to remember.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.780
Have you noticed how simple it is to know

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:38.500
if the decimal is terminating or non-terminating recurring.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.440
All we have to do is write the denominators

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.580
in terms of its prime factors and apply these rules.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.140
Let's generalize these two rules.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.920
If the number is in the form 'p' by 'q',

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:55.060
then all we need to do is reduce 'q' to its prime factors.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:59.880
For terminating decimals, 'q' will be of the form '2 raised to m'

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.040
multiplied by '5 raised to 'n',

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.820
where 'm and n' are integers.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:10.580
It simply means that the prime factors will be either 2 or 5 or both.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:12.420
And how do we generalize 'q',

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.080
for non-terminating recurring decimals?

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:18.760
It's simple, we write it as '2 raised to m'

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:22.440
multiplied by '5 raised to n' multiplied by 'k'.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.400
Where 'k' is a prime factor

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:29.100
other than 2 or 5 and 'm and n' are integers.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.620
It can be 3, it can be 7 or 11 whatever.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.880
If the denominator contains any prime factor other than 2 or 5,

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:41.080
it will be a non-terminating recurring decimal.

